# thread-split.allow — the files that may name engine memory, each with its class, its
# scope and the argument for it (tools/thread-split-check.sh; frame-packet-exchange §10).
#
# DEFAULT-DENY: only a file listed here may name an engine address, include the engine
# header, probe with IsBad*Ptr or add an offset to the main pointer, with comments
# stripped. The list only SHRINKS: a change that converts a file to the packet removes
# its line in the same diff. Adding a
# line loosens the rule for every future edit of that file and is the owner's decision,
# taken in a review — never a session's way past a red build.
#
# Classes, one per line after the path:
#   publisher         game-thread code inside an engine call, at a site that owns the
#                     memory it reads (observers, the packet's fill, detour installers
#                     that byte-match at attach)
#   session-reader    render thread, data that lives for the session and is never
#                     rewritten (GAF banks, the cursor table); NOT fonts
#   fenced            render thread, per-LEVEL assets (model templates, the tile set)
#                     under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence; retired by the asset channel,
#                     the decision after landing 5
#   tooling           trigger-gated readers (peek, catalogues, the GUI snapshot, the
#                     weapon dump, scenario detection); out of the exchange's scope.
#                     THEY RUN ON THE GAME THREAD, from the engine's own flip
#                     (tagpu_gui_hook.c's before_flip) rather than from
#                     tagpu_overlay_draw -- which is what lets them reach the GDI backend
#                     -- so they read engine memory from the thread that owns it.
#   pure-engine-code  render thread executes an engine FUNCTION over our buffers, with
#                     the disassembly range that shows it reads only its arguments and
#                     writes only its destination
#   engine-map        the header the addresses live in (publisher files include it)
#   to-convert:N      render-thread reader of per-frame or per-map engine state, to be
#                     converted in landing N of the migration table
#
# Scope, one word after the class (tools/sim-state-check.sh; inc/tagpu_simreg.h):
#   sim               the simulation's own code: every writable static, heap table and TLS
#                     slot it has is declared to the demo recorder's registry, and its memory,
#                     TLS indices, page changes and levers come through the registry's
#                     wrappers. A source file only: a header's statics belong to the objects
#                     that include it.
#   presentation      everything else: renderers, the UI, the camera, the menu, reclamation,
#                     scenarios and catalogues, the TADR takeover. Out of the recorder's
#                     scope, so marking a file that holds simulation state `presentation` is
#                     a loosening, and the owner's decision.
#
# The argument after `#` is what the reviewer checks, not what the script checks.

inc/tagpu_engine.h       engine-map          presentation  # the addresses, for publisher files only
src/tagpu_packet_pub.c   publisher           presentation  # the frame packet's fill, from DrawGameScreen's `after` at 0x4969D2; the font copy at hook 8; the level-end packet; and LoadMap's join 0x483B53 on the LOADER thread (mapfeat_at_load): LoadMap's own frame, the FeatureDefs 0x421F20 has just counted and the grid's heights LoadMap has just written, read after those writes in LoadMap's own order on its own thread, before the level's first in-play draw; the list it builds is ours, and reaches the game thread only through the interlocked hand-over slot
src/tagpu_markown.c      publisher           presentation  # the marker block's call-site redirects on the game thread; hook 8 latches the font through the publisher
src/tagpu_menu.c         publisher           presentation  # the render-options screen: GUI calls on the game thread from the DrawGameScreen observer
src/tagpu_hud.c          publisher           presentation  # HUD scale: an observer on the loading-screen flip 0x4288D0 gated on the return address 0x498242 writes the viewport rect's R/B/W/H on the game thread; the one word it publishes is its own
src/tagpu_reclaim.c      publisher           presentation  # FreeObjectState and the teardown wrap on the game thread; the fence itself
src/tagpu_gui_hook.c     publisher           presentation  # the UI observers and the flip publisher on the game thread; the probes are on its own arena
src/tagpu_gui_leaves.h   publisher           presentation  # observers on the game thread, all of them. The minimap picture main+0x1426B is alive for the whole level, not only inside BuildMinimapSurface 0x466780, so the packet's publisher decodes it on the level's first in-play draw. Nothing in this file runs on the loader thread
src/tagpu_order.c        publisher           presentation  # the order-marker snapshot at 0x469BFC, game thread: it resolves every unit pointer to an array SLOT and every def and weapon field to a number in the record, so the present half reads the packet's units, GUI colours and palette and dereferences nothing. The GAF sequences it carries are session assets tagpu_gaf.c resolves
src/tagpu_owndraw.c      publisher           presentation  # the composite detours' classifier on the game thread (0x459830/0x459C70)
src/tagpu_fxown.c        publisher           presentation  # effects call-site redirects and leaf detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_featown.c      publisher           presentation  # the feature leaf detour 0x46A610 on the game thread
src/tagpu_terrown.c      publisher           presentation  # the terrain and fog-overlay detours; the fog grid's lazy rebuild on the game thread
src/tagpu_fogwide.c      publisher           presentation  # the wide fog grid: built on the GAME thread inside an in-play draw — at the engine's own fog site when it is ours, else, on the Vulkan renderer, in the publisher's `after` of the same draw — after the level load that allocates the LOS and MAPPED maps and before the teardown (which frees MAPPED; the LOS counters are freed by the next load). It hands over nothing: the packet's publisher copies the grid out of it on this thread, in the same draw, so it needs no hand-over buffer, critical section or retire ring
src/tagpu_patches.c      publisher           sim           # byte patches applied at attach, before the engine runs; and the terrain window bound, which reads the pass's frame, the tile map, the tile set and the OFFSCREEN on every in-play draw, on the game thread, inside 0x483FA0 through its 0x484057 stub; and, not the game thread, the saved-feature restore on the LOADER thread: it reads and writes the feature grid's def words and refreshes the pathing maps around 0x424C00's one call at 0x43265A, and reads the grid and the FeatureDefs where 0x424C00 writes a saved record's state (0x4250C0, 0x425185) -- inside the level load, in the engine's own order on its own thread, before any unit is restored and before the level's first in-play draw; the render thread reads none of it
src/tagpu_suppress.c     publisher           presentation  # the DrawUnit detour's classifier on the game thread
src/tagpu_tracer.c       publisher           presentation  # the draw tracer's detours on the game thread
src/tagpu_cobtrace.c     publisher           sim           # the COB oracle's five sites inside the script engine, game thread
src/tagpu_datakeys.c     publisher           sim           # TADR section C's unit keys. Observers at the unit-data load's start 0x42D2E0, the FBI loader's entry 0x42BF40 and its read site 0x42BF97, and the COB checksum 0x4B6BA0, on the LOADER thread (the level's unit-data load) and on the GAME thread (the console's one-type reload 0x42D1F0): the section the loader holds, through the engine's own reader 0x4C48C0, and each script block's address and length; the records they write are ours. The ghost masks, on the GAME thread inside the in-play packet fill: the def array, MODEL_PTRS, each type's COB (def+0x18E) and template tree, all of the LEVEL, loaded before its first in-play draw and freed by its teardown on this thread, the COB also by a Reload on this thread; every read of a COB is bounded by its recorded length. The weapon keys (C2): their store written on the LOADER thread (emptied at 0x42E310, filled at 0x42E468) with a weapon's +0x111 noradar bit at 0x49E010, and read on the GAME thread by the targeting sites, which clear a slot's target through the engine's ClearTarget 0x48A0F0; nomapweaponalert's hit frame, blink hold and the save record's byte +0xB3 on the GAME thread (the sim tick, the dispatcher's 0x0B case, the unit saver), the hold restored on the LOADER thread inside the same load that emptied it. The render thread reads none of it: the masks reach it as a packet table
src/tagpu_weapons.c      publisher           sim           # the extra-weapons module: sim-side sites on the game thread; its def records written on the LOADER thread inside the unit-data load (emptied at 0x42D2E0's entry, filled at 0x42CEF2), before the level's first in-play tick, and at a console Reload on the game thread (the piece caches forgotten at 0x42BF40's entry, the whole record rewritten at 0x42CEF2 when the FBI opens); the WEAPON_FIRED receiver can also run during a load (the dispatcher 0x453D40 at 0x49852E and 0x49727D), reads those records bounded and can fill one piece cache, in an array never freed; the weapon dump is tooling
src/tagpu_recorder.c     publisher           presentation  # the demo recorder's capture, inside B2's stub at the sim step's unit-tick call 0x4954ED, on the GAME thread between two iterations: the unit array (bounded by the engine's own slot count, checked against its last-slot pointer), a live unit's mover (0x2F bytes the creates allocate and only the destructor frees, nulling +0x00 in the same call) and its two order lists through each node's next (every node made, linked and freed on this thread; a vtable test and a 4096-node cap end the recording rather than read on; a node's owned object read once for its vtable), the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (nothing frees main's), and the declared SIM_STATE rows, each bounded by its declaration; for the header and the harness's dump also the unit-def and weapon names (UNITINFOCount of them, the weapon array's slots), the player names and alliance rows, the game options, the skirmish settings' start mode and map name, and GameingState's TNT path. An id row's pointer is never dereferenced but an order node's owned object: its ID is one more than its row in its table. The event hooks run on the GAME thread inside the engine's own calls (the create's exit, the destructor's entry, the three script broadcasts, the build-done, the state setter, the two replies, the nano query, emitters and handler calls, thirteen owner-only COB starts, and the fire functions' 0x0D and area damage's 0x0E through tagpu_patches.c): they read the call's own arguments and registers no further than the engine does, name a unit only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a call on any other thread is counted and not recorded; the five fire functions' network tests are released while recording, which builds the packet on their own stack and runs the extra weapons' slot-index splice, which writes no simulation state (tagpu_weapons.c, cb_slot_index). The recording ends at the teardown wrap's entry, before the cascade frees the array, and does not start without that wrap. It hands images to the writer thread through a ring; the writer, tagpu_recwriter.c, reads no engine memory and is not listed
src/tagpu_puppet.c       publisher           sim           # the puppet host's table (tagpu_puppet.h): whether this level plays a recording, which records are puppet seats and getter 75's answer for each, SIM_CONTENT (decision D3), written by the seat fill on the GAME thread in the SKIRMISH Start before the loader thread exists and cleared by the teardown wrap on the game thread; read by COB's getter on the game thread and the loader thread, and by the loader's start-unit stub, between those two points. And its gates: prologue gates landed at attach on twelve engine functions (the damage apply 0x489CE0, Send_UnitDeath 0x4864B0, COB's SET 0x480B20 at its second instruction 0x480B26, ATTACH 0x481340 and DROP 0x4813B0, the replies 0x47F780 and 0x47F850, the meteor shower 0x437DE0, typed commands 0x417B50, the debug keys 0x4956C0, the resource transfer 0x464C60 and the save 0x4326B0). A predicate reads nothing but the mode unless the level plays a recording; then, on the thread the engine calls the function on, it reads the call's own arguments and registers, the unit array's bounds from main (the level holds the array until its teardown, whose wrap ends the mode first), a unit's owner byte only once its pointer is a slot by arithmetic, and a COB object's unit as the function itself reads it
src/tagpu_replay.c       publisher           presentation  # playing a recording back, on the GAME thread: the SKIRMISH Start's calls of the seat fill (0x47B092) and of the settings persist (0x47B09C), in the Start handler, read and write the ten player records and their PlayerInfo blocks (main's, which nothing frees), the skirmish settings the Start has just filled from, GameingState's mode and the objective the .ota read at 0x47AF77 armed; the loader's two start-unit calls (0x497963, 0x497ACD) read only the puppet flag, written before the loader thread exists; the step inside B2's stub at the unit-tick call 0x4954ED, between two iterations, reads the level's counts, GameingState's TNT path and GameTime; the observer on the settings save 0x430F00 exchanges the settings with the player's around each save on the save's thread, the game thread (no call of it is on the loader thread), and only from the level's first step, when the loader has read them; the teardown wrap's entry puts the settings back, and its post hook, after 0x491B60 returns on the same thread, writes V's PlayerInfo back. The recording is read by tagpu_recreader.c, which reads no engine memory and is not listed
src/tagpu_scenario.c     tooling             presentation  # both halves on the game thread: the applier on the tick detour, detection on the flip
src/tagpu_cat.c          tooling             presentation  # unit/feature catalogues behind tagpu_units.trigger / tagpu_features.trigger
src/tagpu_gui_snap.c     tooling             presentation  # the gadget-tree snapshot behind tagpu_ui.trigger
src/tagpu_zoom.c         publisher           presentation  # the camera clamp 0x41C3C0, the three target-clamp stubs 0x41C808/0x41C93B (the centre-ons' smooth arms) and 0x41CAF7 (the follow, in the stepper), the GetTPosition guard 0x498EF9 inside the mouse->world 0x498DA0, the debug-overlay call 0x468DBA inside DrawGameScreen, the minimap-rect and ScrollSpeed-save redirects, the command apply at the top of every in-play draw, and the scenario camera's hand-over from the flip observer (written by the next apply, or on the spot where no apply runs): every engine word here is read and written on the game thread; the render half (the levers, the anchor arithmetic, the predicted eye) reads only the packet
src/tagpu_vpwide.c       publisher           presentation  # the 0x498DA0 mouse->world redirect and the clip guard inside DrawGameScreen; the rect is widened and restored from the command apply, game thread; the true rect is read on the game thread only
src/tagpu_native.c       fenced              presentation  # the unit array, the poses, the option bits and the ground heights all come from the packet; what is left is per-LEVEL assets under tagpu_reclaim's teardown fence — the model templates behind MODEL_PTRS (whose base and bound the packet carries). Both fog grids come out of the packet. tagpu_native_owns_unit/owns_obj read the unit record and its def on the GAME thread, for the publisher and the owndraw classifier
src/tagpu_scaffold.c     fenced              presentation  # the units, the feature anchors, the map and sweep dimensions all come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the GAF frames it stamps from are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_render3do.c    fenced              presentation  # there is no write-back, and the shade table and build-state formulas take the packet's copies; the face material helpers still read the model template's face records and their GAF frames, and the atlas decodes the GAF frame of an effects model's face at the address the packet carries when its texels are not painted yet (tagpu_r3d_atlas_texels), per level, under the fence
src/tagpu_feat.c         fenced              presentation  # the anchors, their six height bytes, the map dimensions and the player and option bytes come from the packet; the FeatureDef records and the wreck records it draws GAF frames out of are per-MAP assets under the fence
src/tagpu_text.c         pure-engine-code    presentation  # the glyph blitter 0x4CCF60..0x4CD00E over ONE-GLYPH FONT OBJECTS OF OURS: it reads its arguments and writes its destination. Both text paths hand it a copy — the marker font's glyphs come in the frame packet and the UI's come as bits in the string op — so this file dereferences no engine font and carries no probe
src/tagpu_terr.c         fenced              presentation  # the tile set, the tile map and the height byte of every cell, per map under the fence; the level packet decides its fate
src/tagpu_posebake.c     fenced              presentation  # the per-type geometry bake walks the model template tree on the render thread
src/tagpu_gaf.c          session-reader      presentation  # GAF banks and the cursor table: loaded once, never rewritten. The "fx" bank the effects and particle passes draw from is one of them [VERIFIED 2026-09-12]: 0x429870 loads it and resolves every named sequence into main+0x147AB.., and its only caller is 0x49134D inside 0x491200, whose only caller is WinMain's 0x49EA62 — so those sequences are a SESSION asset, not a per-level one. This file also reads the frame HEADER for its callers (tagpu_gaf_frame_geom / _subframe), so a pass that only places a sprite dereferences nothing of its own
